WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.314
Begin with assets, permitted actions and adversary
capabilities.

00:00:04.314 --> 00:00:10.313
The confidentiality, integrity and availability
goals turn a vague wish for security into

00:00:10.313 --> 00:00:11.189
requirements.

00:00:11.189 --> 00:00:15.772
A guard must identify a requester and check the
requested operation.

00:00:15.772 --> 00:00:22.378
The profile-query example shows why correct checks
also depend on safe interpretation of input and

00:00:22.378 --> 00:00:24.669
complete coverage of access paths.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.972
Use the design principles to make concrete choices.

00:00:27.972 --> 00:00:34.259
Give a component only the rights it needs, deny by
default and keep decision logic understandable.

00:00:34.259 --> 00:00:36.954
Plan what happens when permissions change.

00:00:36.954 --> 00:00:42.215
Combine isolation and independent checks while
keeping the secure workflow usable.

00:00:42.215 --> 00:00:48.310
The backup example exposes the difference between
necessary access and convenient but excessive

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:48.951
authority.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.973
Distinguish authentication from permission and
transport protection from endpoint security.

00:00:54.973 --> 00:01:01.209
A login verifier should use a dedicated salted
password-hashing scheme, not plaintext or a fast

00:01:01.209 --> 00:01:01.668
digest.

00:01:01.668 --> 00:01:06.920
Salt prevents reuse of precomputed tables, while the
hashing cost slows guesses.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:13.483
Moving from repeated passwords to sessions reduces
how widely the account secret must travel through

00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:14.534
the application.

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:20.185
A session reduces repeated password handling but
becomes a credential of its own.

00:01:20.185 --> 00:01:23.255
Follow issuance, use, expiry and revocation.

00:01:23.255 --> 00:01:29.603
Cookies can transport sessions, while structured
tokens need standard integrity protection.

00:01:29.603 --> 00:01:34.836
Examine central validation and cached decisions as
architectural tradeoffs.

00:01:34.836 --> 00:01:41.394
Finally, challenge-response and second factors need
careful protocol design; merely avoiding a

00:01:41.394 --> 00:01:45.440
plaintext password does not guarantee phishing
resistance.
